# agent-box — base egress allowlist.
#
# One DNS name per line. Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are ignored.
# Each name is resolved to A records on every firewall run and the addresses
# are added to the `allowed-domains` ipset. Nothing else may leave the VM.
#
# This file is generic on purpose: it must stay committable to a public
# repository. Anything site-specific (an app under test, a staging host, an
# internal package mirror) belongs in allowlist.local, which lives on the host
# in ~/.config/agent-box/ and is never committed. See docs/decisions.md.
#
# GitHub is NOT listed here. Its address ranges are pulled from
# api.github.com/meta at runtime, which covers github.com, the API, raw
# content and the git endpoints in one go.

# --- Claude Code: model traffic -------------------------------------------
api.anthropic.com
claude.ai
platform.claude.com

# --- Claude Code: the native installer and self-update ---------------------
# claude.ai serves install.sh; downloads.claude.ai serves `latest`,
# manifest.json and the platform binary. Verified by reading install.sh
# (fetched 2026-09-04): DOWNLOAD_BASE_URL="https://downloads.claude.ai/claude-code-releases".
downloads.claude.ai

# --- Package registries the agent may need to install test dependencies ----
registry.npmjs.org
pypi.org
files.pythonhosted.org

# --- The distribution's own package archive --------------------------------
# Ubuntu on arm64 is served from ports.ubuntu.com, not archive.ubuntu.com or
# security.ubuntu.com; both the archive and the security pocket live there.
# Source: /etc/apt/sources.list.d/ubuntu.sources in the Noble arm64 cloud image.
# Needed permanently rather than only during provisioning, because
# `playwright install-deps` shells out to apt and an agent installing a test
# dependency is an ordinary thing to do.
ports.ubuntu.com
# The amd64 archive, for the same reason on an Intel host. Harmless on arm64.
archive.ubuntu.com
security.ubuntu.com

# --- Docker Engine, from Docker's own apt repository -----------------------
# Only reached on an instance created with --docker. Source:
# https://docs.docker.com/engine/install/ubuntu/ (fetched 2026-09-05).
download.docker.com

# --- Container registries --------------------------------------------------
# Docker Hub: the token endpoint, the registry, and the CDN the layer blobs
# are redirected to. Docker Hub uses two blob CDNs and which one a pull is sent
# to is not the client's choice, so both are listed. Source: a real
# `docker pull python:3-alpine` in this guest on 2026-09-05 was redirected to
# production.cloudfront.docker.com and failed until that name was added.
auth.docker.io
registry-1.docker.io
production.cloudfront.docker.com
production.cloudflare.docker.com
# GitHub's registry. ghcr.io itself is on GitHub's meta ranges, but the blob
# host is not, so it is named here. Community-sourced; verify if a pull fails.
ghcr.io
pkg-containers.githubusercontent.com
# Quay, commented out: not needed by default, and every extra name is another
# place an agent could post to. Uncomment the block to pull from quay.io.
# quay.io
# cdn.quay.io
# cdn01.quay.io
# cdn02.quay.io
# cdn03.quay.io
# cdn04.quay.io
# cdn05.quay.io
# cdn06.quay.io

# --- Node and Playwright ---------------------------------------------------
# The Node 22 tarball and its SHASUMS256.txt, fetched by provisioning on an
# instance created with --playwright. Source: https://nodejs.org/dist/.
nodejs.org
# Playwright's browser builds. Each repository's own Playwright version
# downloads its own browsers on first use; PLAYWRIGHT_DOWNLOAD_HOST overrides
# this host. Source: https://playwright.dev/docs/browsers.
#
# This is the worst case for an address-pinning allowlist and worth naming: an
# Azure Front Door endpoint that answers with ONE A record on a near-zero TTL.
# It is the name that showed `dig` and the system resolver returning different
# addresses for the same host in the same second — which is why the firewall
# now resolves through both paths. A download can still land on an address that
# was not in the set; retry, or re-run `agentbox firewall-check`. See "Why the
# allowlist resolves through two paths, and more than once" in
# docs/decisions.md.
#
# Written with a LEADING DOT deliberately, and it is the only reason this host
# works. A bare name is pre-resolved at rebuild and pinned to whatever it
# answered with then; a dot line is a subtree that follows the resolver, so the
# address the guest is about to connect to is the one that gets allowed. For a
# Front Door endpoint on a near-zero TTL that is the difference between working
# and not. The cost is that every subdomain of it is admitted too, which is
# what a dot line means and why it is not the default form.
.cdn.playwright.dev
# And where cdn.playwright.dev SENDS you. Playwright 1.63's
# `playwright install chromium` fetches Chrome for Testing, and
# cdn.playwright.dev answers 307 to
# https://storage.googleapis.com/chrome-for-testing-public/... — a different
# host, so every address of it is off-list and the download dies with
# EHOSTUNREACH in deny mode with nothing naming the redirect. Measured on
# 2026-09-06 by running a real `playwright install chromium` in a --playwright
# box under the standing deny.
#
# This is the third redirect of its kind in this file, after Docker Hub's two
# blob CDNs. The pattern is worth stating: an allowlist of names is an
# allowlist of the names a client is SENT to, not the ones it asks for, and
# only a real download finds the difference. The smoke now downloads a browser
# rather than only installing its system libraries, for exactly that reason.
# Also a dot line, for the same reason and with a wider cost: this is Google's
# object store, so the subtree is every bucket anyone has ever published there.
# It is admitted because Chrome for Testing lives in one of them and the 307
# cannot be followed otherwise. Narrow it to the bucket's own host if that ever
# becomes possible.
.storage.googleapis.com

# dprint's formatter plugins. The dprint binary formats NOTHING on its own: the
# rules for each language are WASM modules it fetches on first use and caches in
# ~/.cache/dprint. Measured in a box before this line existed, with the pinned
# 0.57.4 installed and a project pinning the markdown plugin:
#
#   $ dprint check
#   Error resolving plugin https://plugins.dprint.dev/markdown-0.24.0.wasm:
#     Connection Failed: No route to host (os error 113)      (exit 12)
#
# So without this entry a baseline tool is present and unusable, and a project
# whose CI runs `dprint check` is not reproducible in a box. Admitted as an exact
# name, not a dot line: only this host serves the plugins.
#
# What it costs, stated plainly: like every name here it is an outbound channel
# the agent's shell can also reach. It is a smaller one than pypi.org,
# files.pythonhosted.org, registry.npmjs.org and github.com, already on this list,
# each of which serves arbitrary executable code rather than WASM that dprint runs
# with no filesystem and no network of its own. The remaining exposure is a
# swapped module, which is why guest/conventions.md tells the agent to pin a
# plugin as `url@<sha256>` and guest/project-pins.py reports one that is not.
plugins.dprint.dev

# Deliberately NOT allowed, and disabled by DISABLE_TELEMETRY=1 and
# DISABLE_ERROR_REPORTING=1 in the guest environment instead:
#   sentry.io, statsig.com, statsig.anthropic.com
# Deliberately NOT allowed, because nothing in this VM is an editor:
#   marketplace.visualstudio.com, vscode.blob.core.windows.net,
#   update.code.visualstudio.com
